  "provenance": {
    "pattern": "trip-hop, the dusty boom-bap: a heavy kick on one, the a of two and three-and, a fat snare on two and four with a ghost before the last, lazy swung hats on the ands, a dark bass that sits on the root and falls, and one dark struck chord every two bars far back",
    "knowledge": "public knowledge of the genre: the pattern as drummers, producers and tutorials commonly write it; no recording was sampled, transcribed from audio or mined",
    "note": "Two bars: kick one, two-a, three-and; one, two-a, three-and; the snare on two and four at full weight with a ghost a sixteenth before bar two's four; the hats on the ands only, swung to 0.60 and late by a twenty-fifth of a beat with loose dynamics. The bass holds the root for most of a bar, drops a third and climbs back; a stacked-seventh chord on a dark struck keyboard lands on one every two bars and is left to ring. Played under the Trip Hop tile's spell at 85-95 (round G4), where the broken kit's own break and snare carry it.",
